From 0361d8ffa9393b8024c1bd80bcf241abcea3ba4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hoangvu12 <hggaming91@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 18:48:07 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] Calls to another of the person's computers take the direct
 link wherever they can

direct-rpc-links moved only a message's files and the command relay onto
the direct WebRTC link. Everything else a device asks of another (a
transcript's images, attachment and file reads, git status, terminals,
queues, settings) still crossed the edge's relay, which is often slow.

direct_rpc::Roads is now the one place a call to another device picks its
road: the direct link when the device advertises direct-rpc-v1 and one is
up or opens now, else the relay (and pairing starts, for the next call).
The fallback, the invalidation of a link a call failed on and the log of
road changes are the same for every caller.

- Device-addressed calls (rpc.rs forward, targetDeviceId) go through it,
  unary calls and streams alike: the direct link carries the same frames
  as the relay, so subscribe_checked / subscribe_scoped work on it as
  they do there. A stream on a link that drops ends, as a relay link's
  does, and its subscriber asks again. A read cut off on the direct link
  (Read*, List*, Get*, Search*, Watch*, Subscribe*, DeviceStats) is asked
  again on the relay at once; anything else isn't repeated.
- The doc host's attachment transfers and command relay use it too
  (direct-rpc-links' peer_client, now a call into Roads).
- An open direct link with a stream or a call on it isn't closed as idle.
- Plugins' calls keep to the relay: their host trusts the caller only as
  the device the edge verified on a relay link (reviews, decisions,
  installs from another device; a plugin's deliveries, service and agent
  calls name their sender by it). A direct link's far end is the
  signaling's claim, served as an unverified peer, so those would be
  refused or come from "Another device".
- The host's log says when a direct link starts serving calls from a
  device ("direct RPC link serving calls from this device") and when it
  closes, so either computer's log shows the road.
- Signing out closes the direct links with the relay's.

The guard is unchanged: the same peer_service as the relay, local-only
calls refused, Cloud machines neither open nor serve one.

Tests: device_routing's
a_forwarded_image_read_takes_the_direct_link_when_up_and_the_relay_when_down
and a_forwarded_stream_takes_the_direct_link_when_up_and_the_relay_when_down
(a stand-in pairing in front of B's real direct RPC host);
direct_rpc's calls_that_need_the_verified_caller_keep_to_the_relay and
only_reads_are_asked_again_on_the_relay.
